{
  "rationale": "Budget and round cap are both exhausted (400/400 evals, round 4 of 4), so the only valid choices are stop or infeasible. Infeasible is ruled out by the evidence: 273 of 400 evaluations are feasible, all three constraints are met by real designs (throughput best 273 MSPS vs >=32; max_abs_err best 2.41e-08 vs <=2.44e-04; sys_p99_batch best 0.161 us vs <=0.44 us), and the feasible Pareto front holds 37 designs spanning luts_plus_ffs 1165..4230 and accuracy_bits 12..25.3, i.e. it covers both objective ranges rather than a corner. All four registry families were explored, so there is no unexplored family to add, and the front already reaches the registry limits on data_width (16..28) and near the HV reference points (4000 area, 12 bits). The last round's hypervolume gain was only +1.6% (1.997e+04), indicating the front has converged, so further refinement would not be a good use of a budget that no longer exists. The spec's selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) picks the pipelined_m [data_width=16 n_iter=15 angle_guard=2 frac_guard=2 rounding=trunc m=4] design at luts_plus_ffs=1165, which satisfies every constraint (throughput 97.8 MSPS, max_abs_err 2.41e-04 <= 2.44e-04, sys_p99_batch 0.161 us <= 0.44 us). Caveat for the L2 stage: that cheapest design's error margin is thin (2.41e-04 against a 2.44e-04 limit) and its L1 system bound is optimistic, so if L2 simulation rejects it the next front points (e.g. luts_plus_ffs=1325 at 12.8 bits, or 1494 at 14.2 bits) are the fallbacks. Stopping with the converged feasible front is the correct final decision.",
